   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Yes, that&#39;s correct. Stan&#39;s univariate distributions do not accept matrices. (yet?)<br><br><br></div>Daniel<br><br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Fri, Feb 6, 2015 at 10:12 AM, Andrew Gelman <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">gel...@stat.columbia.edu</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">Hi, I tried this:<br>\r\n  y1 ~ normal(y1_pred, sigma_y);<br>\r\nin which y1 was a matrix, y1_pred was a matrix, and sigma_y is a scalar.  But it did not work.  Is that right?<br>\r\n</blockquote></div></div>"
